 established in 1989 when the supreme  court decided the case of descheney vs  winnebago county department of social  services  in that case in 1982 joshua deshaney was  a four-year-old boy living with his  father randy in wisconsin randy had  primary custody of the child and had  recently gone through a second divorce  joshua's stepmother told the winnebago  county department of social services  that randy abused his son randy denied  it and the county took no action to  remove the child however in 1983 joshua  was seen at the er for bruises the er  doctor notified social services and  joshua was removed from randy's custody  for a few days randy wasn't charged and  the child was returned to his custody a  social worker was assigned to visit the  home and in 1984 the social worker  visited the house 20 times noting that  the child had bumps bruises and injuries  on his head the social worker also  documented that randy failed to attend  his regular counseling sessions or  enrolled joshua in preschool and during  this time joshua ended up in the er  twice with suspicious injuries the  social worker said that she feared for  his life but took no action to remove  him from the home and unfortunately in  march of 1984 one day after the social  worker's visit randy beat joshua so hard  that he fell into a coma joshua had  severe brain damage and was permanently  paralyzed the state then placed joshua  in an institution where he would remain  for the rest of his life joshua  duchene's mother sued the winnebago  county department of social services  alleging that the department had  deprived joshua of his liberty interest  in bodily integrity and violation of his  rights under the substantive component  of the 14th amendment's due process  clause by failing to intervene to  protect him against his father's  violence now the due process clause of  the 14th amendment declares that no  state shall deprive any person of life  liberty or property without due process  of law the shani argued that the county  had a responsibility to protect joshua  because it knew randy abused him the  social worker had tracked the abuse on  paper for over a year the shaney argued  that through the social workers visits  the state had established a special  relationship with joshua that created a  responsibility to protect him from known  dangers then chief justice of the  supreme court william rehnquist wrote  the majority opinion which found that  the due process clause only applies to  states not to private citizens since  randy was the one who beat his son the  court reasoned that the state didn't  directly cause joshua's injuries and the  majority also rejected the argument that  the state made joshua more vulnerable  the purpose of the due process clause  was to protect people from the state not  to ensure that the state protected them  from each other the court held that the  14th amendment did not grant any citizen  a guarantee of quote minimal levels of  safety and security the court also said  that people have no right to  governmental aid the court held that the  due process clause would only apply if  the state had assumed custody of a  person against their will and then  failed to provide for their safety and  general well-being justice brennan and  the two other dissenters sought another  way they believed that since the state  had acted through the social work  department they assumed the  responsibility for the child's safety  the dissenter said that if the state had  never intervened then others in joshua's  life like his mother might have taken  other actions to get him away from randy  and basically in the duchesne case what  happens is that a mother is worried that  her son who's in the custody of the  father  is being abused by the father she goes  to the police  they send social workers out a social  worker visits 20 times sees lots and  lots of evidence of abuse  ultimately the social services doesn't  do enough and the child is horribly and  permanently injured by the the father  in large part because social services  allowed  the child to remain with the husband now  there are obvious differences between  what happened in uvalde and a social  worker who fails to remove a child from  a violent parent but that takes us to  the 2005 case of the town of castle rock  colorado versus gonzalez where the  supreme court applied the same rationale  to a case involving the police now in  that case jessica gonzalez had asked for  a restraining order against her  estranged husband there was a colorado  law that made domestic violence  restraining orders mandatory whenever  one party felt threatened and the state  trial court issued an order prohibiting  the husband from seeing miss gonzalez or  their three daughters unless it was  through a pre-arranged visit now just  one month later the husband kidnapped  the three children ms gonzalez had  direct evidence that he had the kids  because he confirmed it on the phone she  begged the police to search for her  husband right away since she violated  the court order now the castle rock  police department told ms gonzalez that  it could do nothing and told her she had  to wait until 10 pm to see if her  husband brought the daughters home  at 10 pm the police told jessica to wait  until midnight at midnight she went to  the police station to beg them to arrest  her husband an officer took the report  and then went to dinner a few hours  later gonzalez's husband went to the  police station and opened fire police  killed him in the melee and when they  searched his truck they found the three  girls dead in the back gonzalez's  husband had murdered all three that  night miss gonzales sued the castle rock  police department for violating her due  process rights and the 10th circuit  court of appeals found that she had a  protected property interest in the  enforcement of the terms of her  restraining order which the police had  violated but the supreme court reversed  that decision ruling that gonzalez had  no constitutionally protected property  interest in the enforcement of the  restraining order and therefore could  not claim that the police had violated  her right to due process justice antonin  scalia wrote the majority opinion he  reasoned that the state law didn't  entitle anyone to mandatory action by  police even though the issue into the  restraining order was mandatory under  colorado law restraining orders provide  the grounds for arresting a person the  constrained to the order but that  doesn't mean that the recipient can  force the police to act scalia wrote  that under the colorado law police have  discretion to decide whether to enforce  an order  and if so how they would do so the court  concluded that since colorado had not  created such an entitlement to mandatory  action gonzales had no property interest  and the due process clause was therefore  inapplicable the court ultimately  concluded that unless a legislature  specifically said  we're creating this right and if we  breach it the private citizen can then  sue the government then the citizen is  out of luck and as one court put it  quote law enforcement officers often  consider themselves responsible for  protecting the public and deterring  criminal activity however the legal  doctrine that specifies the legal duty  of law enforcement officers holds that  they have a duty to the public but not  to individual citizens  this is what's known as the public duty  doctrine the doctrine means that the  officers don't have a duty to protect  citizens from harm by a third party in  other words there is no clear duty to  protect individual citizens now that  being said the duscheny decision  recognized two possible exceptions to  the general rule that the state has no  duty to protect people from harm the  first is what's called the special  relationship exception which holds the  the constitution imposes upon the state  of duty of care towards individuals who  are in its custody as is usually  summarized when the state takes a person  into its custody and holds him there  against his will the constitution  imposes upon it a corresponding duty to  assume some responsibility for his  safety and well-being so when the state  has a special relationship with someone  there may be a duty to protect if this  relationship exists the state official  must take reasonable steps to ensure the  safety and care of that person and to  safeguard them against foreseeable risks  however most courts say that the only  time the state forms a special  relationship is through incarceration  institutionalization or other similar  restraint of personal liberty and most  courts reject the theory that people  waiting to be rescued are in police  custody and control this is particularly  true in the fifth circuit and perhaps  the most analogous fifth circuit case is  salas vs carpenter where a county clerk  was taken hostage at the courthouse by  her abusive boyfriend and during the  hostage negotiation the sheriff ordered  that this city swat and hostage  negotiation teams leave the courthouse  he then replaced them with county  personnel not trained for swat or  hostage negotiations  the man killed the county clerk and the  fifth circuit sided with the share  finding that he did not violate her  constitutional rights by using untrained  negotiators the court said quote  generally the absence of a special  relationship defeats any due process  claim based on a failure to provide  protective services the victim was not  held in state custody or otherwise  prevented by the state from caring for  herself this was a failed rescue effort  and it's easy to see how the fifth  circuit which covers texas would  probably make the same call in the  hypothetical uvalde case the police were  doing something they were securing the  parking lot and stopping parents from  getting inside where they might get hurt  and just because the effort failed and  failed badly probably wouldn't change  the fifth circuit's analysis but under  the descending case there is a second  exception and that is called the  state-created danger exception and under  that theory some courts have recognized  that state officials have a duty to  protect individuals from harm when their  actions created or exacerbated a danger  to that individual but here it seems  like this exception wouldn't apply the  police didn't help at least not in the  way that most people wanted them to help  but at the same time they also did not  create the situation itself now those of  you that have been paying attention  might have come up with a legal theory  that might differentiate what happened  in uvalde texas from the prior precedent  and that is that  children are mandated to go to school  often those schools are provided by the  government and in this case  this massacre occurred at a public  school so aren't the children within the  custody of the government and doesn't  the government then have an obligation  to provide for the health and safety of  the children especially a school that  has a police officer and a school  district that has its own police  department  well it's complicated but it probably  doesn't turn out the way that you might  want because unfortunately this is not  the first time there has been a massacre  on school campus and a relatively recent  case is almost directly on subject and  that is the case arising out of the  parkland situation in late 2020 the 11th  circuit court of appeals cited the  castle rock and shaney cases and  dismissed a lawsuit brought by the  victims of the parkland florida shooting  at the marjory stoneman douglas high  school now there the students allege  that the county sheriff and deputies  incompetently responded to an active  shooter situation violating their due  process rights the deputies knew that  the shooter was  a danger to the community and school  officials had also known that the  shooter was deeply troubled and had made  threats the plaintiffs allege that the  the deputies had failed to intervene  during the shooting and they also noted  that one deputy prevented ems from  providing life-saving care and blocking  them from entering the school during the  shooting the 11th circuit court of  appeals which is one step below the  supreme court found that a plaintiff  could only allege a substantive due  process claim in two situations first  when the person was in the government's  custody and second when a government  official's conduct is arbitrary or  shocks the conscience now first the  court held that the children were not in  police custody and as far as shocking  the conscience the court held that in an  active shooter situation police have to  make split-second decisions in the midst  of chaos in this situation quote an  official's conduct will shock the  conscience only when it stems from a  purpose to cause harm that means that a  plaintiff can only sue if they can show  that the government officer purposefully  caused harm if the police officer didn't  intend hurt anyone then they can't be  sued and with respect to whether the  children were in government custody  the court held quote it is well  established that school children are not  in a custodial relationship with the  state a custodial relationship exists  only if the government places  limitations upon individuals ability to  act on his own behalf that are similar  in kind to incarceration or other forms  of involuntary confinement school  attendance even when compelled by  truancy and compulsory attendance laws  is not similar in kind to incarceration  or institutional confinement ordinarily  there are no custodial relationships in  the public school system even if  officials are aware of potential dangers  or have expressed an intent to provide  aid on school grounds  and in the parkland case the biggest  controversy was about scott peterson a  broward county sheriff's deputy and  school resource officer peterson stayed  outside of a school building for over 40  minutes as students and faculty members  were gunned down and the state of  florida charged peterson with neglect  under a statute that applies to  caregivers now this is generally a novel  interpretation of law which usually  applies to parents or adult household  members or teachers now peterson will  stand trial on this criminal case but  the 11th circuit dismissed the civil  case against peterson for the same  reasons it dismissed the cases against  other defendants that these students  were not in a custodial relationship  with the officials even the school's  armed resource officer  a lot of people disagree with this  particular ruling and it can be solved  with legislation  but as it currently stands there is no  inherent cause of action based on the  constitution that would allow  parents and teachers and victims to sue  the police for inaction at uvalde and  unfortunately that is the current state  of the law now if you'd like to actually  do some good one thing i can recommend  is a company i work with called tab for  a cause which lets you raise money for  the charity of your choice just by  opening tabs while you're browsing the  internet tab for cause is a google  chrome extension that allows you to pick  a charity of your choice then every time  you open up a new tab instead of a blank  page it displays a new customizable tab  that displays a couple of tiny ads and  the money that you generate through  those ads is then donated to the charity  of your choice and you can see how much  you're generating in real time i've  worked with tab for cause for years and  legal eagle viewers have actually raised  over five thousand dollars for charity  just by themselves and it's great  because tab for causes finances are 100  transparent so you can see for yourself  how much you've raised and where it's  gone so if you're in the mood to do  something good for this world all you  have to do